% ============================================================================
% planets — each planet's order from the Sun (adj-facts-stdlib, ASTRONOMY).
% ============================================================================
% An astronomy facts library — an early science fact: the order of the
% planets, counting out from the Sun — the thing a child chants ("My Very Easy
% Method Just Speeds Up Naming Planets"). Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? planet_order(earth, $N)      % 3  (Earth is the third planet from the Sun)
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `planet_order(planet, order)` carrying the NASA citation, so the lookup above
% is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the position WITH its
% source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a non-planet (e.g.
% pluto, reclassified a dwarf planet in 2006, is deliberately NOT a row here).
% The `order` value is a NUMBER, so a recalled position composes into arithmetic
% (how many planets lie between Mars and Saturn? 6 − 4 − 1 = 1).
%
% Provenance: the citable artifact is NASA's Solar System Planets hub at the
% `locator`, and that ONE page carries a sentence for each planet. Each row now
% carries ITS OWN SENTENCE (RS-5e per-row provenance, #14986). The envelope's
% `trust authoritative` — the space agency's primary reference — is inherited by
% seven of the eight rows; Mercury overrides it, for the reason set out below. No row restates `locator`: one page, and
% a row inherits every field it does not write. Order is the canonical eight
% major planets only, Mercury (nearest) → Neptune (farthest).
%
% This paragraph used to say every order "is confirmed by its own NASA page".
% The spans below are all from the HUB page, not from eight per-planet pages;
% the per-planet pages were not fetched and nothing here rests on them.
%
% SEVEN OF EIGHT ROWS ARE READ, ONE IS REASONED, and the tier says which.
% Measured 2026-09-13 UTC with a raw fetch, each of the eight spans occurs
% EXACTLY ONCE in the raw HTML and once in the rendered text (inline tags
% removed with no separator, block tags as breaks). Seven of them literally
% state their row's ordinal, e.g. "the fifth planet from the Sun".
% NEPTUNE'S IS THE MARGINAL ONE of those seven: "the eighth and most
% distant planet in our solar system" gives the ordinal word but takes the
% from-the-Sun axis from "most distant" rather than from the phrase itself.
% It is still a literal statement of position 8, and it is named here rather
% than quietly counted with the other six.
% MERCURY'S DOES NOT STATE ITS ORDINAL AT ALL: the page says "the planet nearest to the
% Sun", never "the first planet". That row is read off "nearest" plus the
% ordering of a second span, "The first four planets from the Sun are Mercury,
% Venus, Earth, and Mars.", in which Mercury is named first — a reading, not a
% quotation — so it alone carries `trust inferred`, the same treatment the
% tropic rows get in `geography/reference-lines.adj`.
%
% Controls for that measurement: a nonsense span scores zero; and a near-miss
% of each of the eight (its ordinal word changed) scores zero, so a count of
% one is not loose matching. The en dashes in the Earth sentence are the page's
% own U+2013 and survive byte-exact.
% (See ../README.md; ADJ-TABLES.md RS-5e;
% feedback_nothing_human_authored, feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table planet_order {
    columns planet, order_from_sun

    row (mercury, 1) {
        % The ONE row the page does not state literally: it
        % says "nearest to the Sun", not "the first planet". Position 1
        % is READ OFF that plus the ordering of the second
        % span, where Mercury is named first. A reading, so
        % `trust inferred` -- the same treatment the tropic
        % rows get in geography/reference-lines.adj.
        source "Mercury is the planet nearest to the Sun, and the smallest planet in our solar system."
        trust inferred
        cites "The first four planets from the Sun are Mercury, Venus, Earth, and Mars." locator "https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/"
    }
    row (venus, 2) { source "Venus is the second planet from the Sun, and the sixth largest planet." }
    row (earth, 3) { source "Earth – our home planet – is the third planet from the Sun, and the fifth largest planet." }
    row (mars, 4) { source "Mars is the fourth planet from the Sun, and the seventh largest planet." }
    row (jupiter, 5) { source "Jupiter is the fifth planet from the Sun, and the largest planet in our solar system." }
    row (saturn, 6) { source "Saturn is the sixth planet from the Sun, the second largest planet in our solar system." }
    row (uranus, 7) { source "Uranus is the seventh planet from the Sun, and the third largest planet in our solar system." }
    row (neptune, 8) { source "Neptune is the eighth and most distant planet in our solar system." }

    % The envelope is the FRAMING span -- what defends the table as
    % a whole: that there are eight planets and that these are they.
    % Until #14986 it was the VENUS sentence, which is one ROW's
    % evidence, so a recall of Jupiter quoted a sentence about Venus.
    source "Our solar system has eight planets: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, and Neptune."
    locator "https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/planets/"
    trust authoritative
}
